    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d249">

                <pb facs="0305" n="291" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg291"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.00/2418c: Circular telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to Certain Diplomatic
                        Representatives in the American Republics </hi><anchor n="56"
                        xml:id="d249-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-25T12:00:00-04:00">February 25,
                            1944—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>For your information and possible use in conversations you may have with
                    officials of the government to which you are accredited the following telegram
                    sent at 1 a.m. today has been received from the American Embassy in Buenos
                    Aires: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I understand that President Ramírez<anchor n="57" xml:id="d249-f2"/> has
                            presented resignation on grounds of health, apparently under pressure
                            from nationalist extremist elements in Army. Situation at moment is
                            extremely confused, but it would appear that these elements have staged
                            successful counter move against those Ramírez was reported to have under
                            consideration in my 517, February 24, 6 p.m.<anchor n="58"
                                xml:id="d249-f3"/> General Farrell,<anchor n="59" xml:id="d249-f4"/>
                            Colonel Perón<anchor n="60" xml:id="d249-f5"/> and other officers are
                            reported in continuous conference at War Ministry. Colonel Velasco has
                            assumed charge of <pb facs="0306" n="292" xml:id="pg292"/> capital
                            police headquarters and a Lieutenant Colonel Lagos of Posts and
                            Telegraphs.</p>
                        <p>“A group of approximately 50 army personnel has moved into Union
                            Telefónica Building, apparently to control telephone censorship.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d249-f1" n="56" type="source" xml:id="d249-f1-content">
                <p>Sent to Brazil, Peru, Chile, Uruguay, Paraguay, Cuba, Ecuador, Venezuela,
                    Colombia, and Mexico.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d249-f2" n="57" xml:id="d249-f2-content">
                <p>Gen. Pedro P. Ramírez. For correspondence on relations between the Ramírez regime
                    and the United States, see <ref target="#pg228">pp. 228</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d249-f3" n="58" xml:id="d249-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d249-f4" n="59" xml:id="d249-f4-content">
                <p>Gen. Edelmiro Farrell, Minister of War under Ramírez.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d249-f5" n="60" xml:id="d249-f5-content">
                <p>Col. Juan Domingo Perón, Director of the National Department of Labor.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
